WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.534
-Ewan, it is --
ah, look at you, buddy.

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:04.170
You look great.
-How are you?

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.739
-It's good to see you.
-I am happy to see you.

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:08.441
-Where were you when
everything shut down?

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:09.742
Were you working on anything
at the time?

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:11.010
-I was in New York.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.545
I was working on the series
called "Halston."

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:15.715
And I'd been in New York.
We'd shot about one episode

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:18.384
out of five, and bits
of two or three.

00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:20.653
So we were less than
halfway through it.

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:22.755
-Oh.
-And then, you know,

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:24.723
we were shooting and we were
hearing all this talk

00:00:24.723 --> 00:00:27.559
on set about other
films being shut down.

00:00:27.559 --> 00:00:30.864
And we're thinking, "Why are we
not being shut down?

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:32.631
What -- what --"

00:00:32.631 --> 00:00:34.333
And then of course,
it came one Friday.

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:35.902
They shut us down --
literally.

00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:37.704
But we thought, at that stage,

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.305
it was going to be
for two or three weeks.

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:40.906
-I totally agree.
Yeah.

00:00:40.906 --> 00:00:42.474
-You know, it's like
two, three weeks, go home.

00:00:42.474 --> 00:00:44.744
So I just left a bunch
of my stuff in New York

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:47.413
and I flew back here to L.A.,

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:49.249
and here we are
six months later.

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:51.050
It's unbelievable, isn't it?

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:54.720
-What kind of things you been
doing while you've been home?

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.156
-Well, you know, I've
just been keeping busy, dude.

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:59.859
I did a whole bunch of stuff
that I've been meaning to do

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:03.296
around the place, like things
that you go, "I'll get to that,"

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:05.564
picking up a tree that fell over

00:01:05.564 --> 00:01:08.167
or I lost a trailer
down the hill.

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:09.835
I had to get --
I towed that out.

00:01:09.835 --> 00:01:12.171
Things that have been on
my to-do list for years.

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:13.940
You know, played
a lot of guitar.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:18.144
I actually -- I found you
playing "Render" with Blur

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:20.380
on the interwebs.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.615
-You saw that?
-Oh, my God.

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.884
I watched it over and over.
It's so good.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:27.487
-How great are --
I just asked them to do it.

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:30.323
They were here and I go,
"I love 'Tender' so much.

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:31.791
Do you want to --"

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:34.193
How did you stumble on that one?
-I don't know.

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:35.662
I was just trying to learn
the chords for it.

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:38.031
And I was looking on YouTube
to see -- you know,

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:40.032
I was watching them play it,
and then I saw you guys.

00:01:40.032 --> 00:01:41.934
I was thinking, "Wait a minute."
So looked at it,

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.402
and then I kept watching
you over and over.

00:01:43.402 --> 00:01:45.371
Just -- what I loved about it

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:48.508
is you just looked like you were
having so much fun.

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:49.943
It was the best.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:51.844
-It was great --
-Your were playing with Blur.

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:54.847
-If you look at
Damon's face,

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:57.382
he starts laughing because
he's looking at

00:01:57.382 --> 00:01:59.418
how much fun I am having.

00:01:59.418 --> 00:02:01.220
And I'm hitting harmonies
and stuff, he's like --

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.022
-You're doing the high stuff.
You were so into it.

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.790
-I'm trying and he's
laughing at me.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.660
-[ Speaks indistinctly ]
-[ Laughing ]

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.996
Oh, that's great, man.
-It's so cool.

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:11.130
-We got to do --
-It's so cool.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.898
-We got to do a duet
when you come back.

00:02:12.898 --> 00:02:14.634
-Oh, I'd love that.
I'd love to --

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.569
Any excuse, really,
to get the guitar out.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:19.272
You know, I'd love it.
-Yeah.

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:21.341
-Last time you were here,
you'd just finished

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:24.243
a motorcycle trip
with your buddy, Charley.

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:26.245
-Charley. Yeah. Yeah.
-Now it's a series

00:02:26.245 --> 00:02:27.780
on Apple TV+.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.717
"Long Way Up,"
it debuts Friday.

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:33.119
What can we expect for this?

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:35.388
-We did a couple of other
big trips in the past

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.323
called "Long Way Round"
and "Long Way Down."

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:39.659
And we -- in 2004,
we rode from London

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:41.227
all the way around
to New York, east.

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:42.895
-I remember, yeah.
-And that was epic.

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.931
And then we did
the African continent

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:48.868
from the top of Scotland
to Cape-Town in 2007.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:51.471
And, you know, there's been
this long gap in between,

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:54.140
but we always planned
to do the Americas.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.709
And so we started, we --
we did an odd --

00:02:57.709 --> 00:03:00.212
we prepped it al
in L.A. and London

00:03:00.212 --> 00:03:02.381
and then shipped everything
down to Ushuaia

00:03:02.381 --> 00:03:04.784
in the bottom of
Tierra del Fuego to start,

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:06.786
which was such a --

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:08.354
We had to come a long way up,
you know,

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:09.922
'cause we'd already done
"Long Way Down."

00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:12.659
The common sense thing would be
to leave L.A. and ride south,

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:15.061
but 'cause we had to call it
"Long Way Up,"

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:16.795
we had to start down there.
-Down, and go up.

00:03:16.795 --> 00:03:18.564
-Then we shipped
everything down there

00:03:18.564 --> 00:03:21.467
and we rolled all the way back
up to L.A., and it was amazing.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.736
And we did it all
on electric motorcycles.

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:26.973
We decided to try and do
this one on electric bikes.

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.775
And Harley-Davidson took
their beautiful

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:31.110
LiveWire motorcycle
and built

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.846
these two prototypes into
sort of adventure bikes for us.

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:36.148
They raised the suspension,

00:03:36.148 --> 00:03:37.416
or they lengthened
the suspension,

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:38.651
they raised the bikes.

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.519
Put different seats on
and panniers

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:44.023
and a screen and we had these
proper dual sports bikes.

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:45.491
It was wicked.

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:46.792
-You said that this was
the most challenging

00:03:46.792 --> 00:03:49.628
motorcycle trip
that you've ever done.

00:03:49.628 --> 00:03:53.298
What was one of the more
challenging part of it?

00:03:53.298 --> 00:03:55.968
-I think the challenge was
the charging of the bikes.

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:59.038
You know, we're in the cusp of,

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:00.740
I know, electric cars
are everywhere.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.875
There are electric charging
stations for cars

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:04.176
in most cities.

00:04:04.176 --> 00:04:05.845
Especially in America,
it's quite easy to do,

00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:07.212
to navigate.

00:04:07.212 --> 00:04:09.882
But in the Atacama Desert

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:11.684
or Patagonia, for instance,

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:13.619
there aren't any
charging stations...

00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:15.721
-Of course.
-...so we had to sort of

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:17.223
do the best we could.

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:18.924
It was pretty stressful,
the charging.

00:04:18.924 --> 00:04:21.527
But it added a certain
excitement to the trip,

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:22.862
a different kind of
adventure, you know.

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.029
It was good.

00:04:24.029 --> 00:04:27.333
-How do you like
electric versus gas powered?

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.835
-I love it. I love --
I love riding them.

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:31.770
They're really fast.
I mean, they're --

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.740
Away from the lights,
they are so much faster

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.643
than a petrol bike, and...
-Really?

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:39.111
-They're just --
they're sort of --

00:04:39.111 --> 00:04:40.546
For a touring bike,
they're quite nice

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:41.948
'cause they don't make
any noise,

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.816
and there's no
mechanical vibrations,

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:45.384
so you really feel the road.

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:46.818
There's sort of a --
sort of tranquility

00:04:46.818 --> 00:04:48.187
to riding them,
which is nice.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.423
You ride them a bit slower
because

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:52.291
you got to try and get
the most miles

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:53.859
out of the battery
as you can.

00:04:53.859 --> 00:04:55.228
-Yeah.
-And so you don't want to be

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:56.462
setting at 90 miles an hour,

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:58.530
but that lends itself
for touring anyway.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.232
You want to be able
to take in the landscape

00:05:00.232 --> 00:05:03.269
and look at the llamas
as you go by, you know?

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:05.238
And also, the llamas
don't run away.

00:05:05.238 --> 00:05:06.839
So when you come up
on a petrol bike

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:10.042
to horses or some wildlife,
they scatter.

00:05:10.042 --> 00:05:11.677
But when you come up
on an electric bike

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:14.280
to the llamas, they just
sort of check you out.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.581
They don't run off.

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:16.982
-I wouldn't even think of that.
You're right.

00:05:16.982 --> 00:05:19.584
I remember I was lucky enough
to go on a hot-air balloon

00:05:19.584 --> 00:05:21.921
ride...
-Yeah.

00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:24.356
-...in Africa,

00:05:24.356 --> 00:05:27.025
and when we went over the
animals, you're just floating,

00:05:27.025 --> 00:05:28.427
and it's quiet,
so the animals

00:05:28.427 --> 00:05:29.828
don't even really
know you're there.

00:05:29.828 --> 00:05:31.631
They just think you're a bird,
so you're just like,

00:05:31.631 --> 00:05:33.966
"Oh, my goodness,
this is unbelievable."

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:35.268
-Amazing.
-So that's probably

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:37.035
kind of the same thing --
it's not as loud,

00:05:37.035 --> 00:05:38.971
so once they do --
[ Imitates grinding ]

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:40.239
...then they ran
and they go...

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:41.641
-Yeah.
-Then you're like, alright.

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:43.142
Then it wasn't as peaceful.

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:45.944
Hot-air ballooning's not as
peaceful as everyone says it is.

00:05:45.944 --> 00:05:48.647
-The landings aren't quite
as peaceful as --

00:05:48.647 --> 00:05:49.882
The landings are
pretty terrifying.

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:51.383
-Dude, it was awful.
-Sort of like --

00:05:51.383 --> 00:05:52.885
It's so hit-and-miss,
isn't it?

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:55.087
It's sort of like, well,
I hope it's gonna be okay.

00:05:55.087 --> 00:05:56.589
-Yeah, we're all tumbling
out of a basket.

00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:58.958
I go, "This is not
how I want to go."

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:02.027
-Dragged across the Savanna
being chased by leopards.

00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:03.362
-[ Laughs ]
-Oh, my God.

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:05.031
[ Laughter ]

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:07.733
-That must have unbelievable
just listening to the tire

00:06:07.733 --> 00:06:09.668
on the road, and just going,

00:06:09.668 --> 00:06:11.270
"We're going, man,
we're cruising."

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:13.005
-Yeah, I mean,
we got down there,

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:14.506
and we're not very good --

00:06:14.506 --> 00:06:16.742
we're not very clever
at planning, Charley and I.

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:18.543
We like looking at bikes
and picture--

00:06:18.543 --> 00:06:20.612
You know, we love
buying the kit,

00:06:20.612 --> 00:06:22.448
getting the right panniers
and the right gloves

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:24.183
and the right gloves and --
-Yeah.

00:06:24.183 --> 00:06:25.818
-That's what we're really into,
all the kit,

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:26.986
you know, the stuff.

00:06:26.986 --> 00:06:28.253
But the actual planning
of the trip,

00:06:28.253 --> 00:06:30.089
we're a bit lame at,
so we go down there

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:32.859
to Tierra del Fuego,
and it was a blizzard,

00:06:32.859 --> 00:06:34.526
and we were snowed in
for four days

00:06:34.526 --> 00:06:37.763
before we could leave,
'cause it was still winter.

00:06:37.763 --> 00:06:39.232
And I spoke to my dad
on the phone, he was like,

00:06:39.232 --> 00:06:40.532
"Well, you might have looked
at the weather

00:06:40.532 --> 00:06:42.201
before you left, son,"
you know.

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:44.470
I'm thinking, yeah,
he's got a point.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:45.671
I should have had a look
at the weather

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:47.540
because it was
blizzard conditions.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:49.608
-You're like, "I'm too busy
thinking of the bike

00:06:49.608 --> 00:06:51.477
and the gloves and thing
and the --" yeah.

00:06:51.477 --> 00:06:53.946
-"I got really nice panniers,
Dad, look at my panniers."

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:55.815
But...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:55.815 --> 00:06:58.351
But we waited for a break
in the weather,

00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:00.453
and on the fifth day
it stopped snowing a bit,

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:02.688
and we just left,
we just got out on the road.

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:03.889
It was fun.

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:05.825
-I want to show everyone
a clip.

00:07:05.825 --> 00:07:07.960
Here is a look
at "Long Way Up"

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.096
on Apple TV+,
check it out.

00:07:10.096 --> 00:07:12.465
-Got to hold on
through your pillar.

00:07:13.999 --> 00:07:17.269
I once was in a taxi cab
in New York,

00:07:17.269 --> 00:07:20.372
and a Hell's Angel came up
to the car like this,

00:07:20.372 --> 00:07:21.974
put his hand through
the window and he said,

00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:24.610
"Take me to the
Hell's Angels club."

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:27.113
Taxi guy was like, "Okay."

00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:31.149
Put your rear window down
and I'll hold on through there.

00:07:31.149 --> 00:07:33.652
Go slowly.
Okay, slowly.

00:07:33.652 --> 00:07:35.187
-Hold the other way,
maybe, Ewan.

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:36.489
-No, I'm good, I got it.
-You got it?

00:07:36.489 --> 00:07:37.723
-Yeah.

00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:39.825
Slowly, slowly.
That's it, good.

00:07:39.825 --> 00:07:41.160
-Okay.
-Good.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.929
-...faster?
-Yeah, go a bit faster.

00:07:43.929 --> 00:07:45.798
Ooh, yeah, that's good.

00:07:45.798 --> 00:07:47.033
Good, good, good.

00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:48.534
This is working,
it's working.

